<Project>

    <PropertyGroup>
        <_SlideRuleAnalyzerProject>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../src/SlideRule.Analyzers/SlideRule.Analyzers.csproj</_SlideRuleAnalyzerProject>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- The three assemblies the compiler loads together, which is how the package ships them: an analyzer's
         dependencies are resolved from the analyzer paths it was handed and from nowhere else. The analyzer
         project names them, and the last call below takes them from it, so neither their folder nor their
         list is restated here.

         Taken as plain Analyzer items rather than reached through a ProjectReference carrying
         OutputItemType="Analyzer". Measured: a project reference is followed into the workspace whatever its
         ReferenceOutputAssembly says, so that shape put the analyzer and the extraction library into every
         example's checked universe — the survey of the Meridian example grew from six projects to eight — and
         an example's universe must hold its own code and nothing of the tool checking it.

         What the project reference used to do, and all it is still wanted for: the three files exist by the
         time anything compiles against them, so a clean clone that builds an example solution before it
         builds anything else still works.

         Restore is its own call under a fixed extra property. A project evaluated before its restore wrote
         project.assets.json is the evaluation the build call would otherwise be served from, so the two calls
         must not share one; and the property is a fixed value rather than one per caller so that every
         example project reaching this target in a parallel build shares a single cached execution instead of
         racing in the same obj directory. TargetFramework is dropped for a neighbouring reason: inherited
         from a multi-targeted caller it would build this project for a framework it does not target, and
         the last call would hand back that build's files.

         One limit comes with it. A rebuild of an example solution (the Rebuild target, which the
         no-incremental switch selects) cleans the contract library, which every example reaches through
         its spec project, while this target asks for the same library through a request the outer build
         cannot order against. Measured: under a parallel build that fails now and then with CS0006. A
         plain build does not, from a clean tree or an old one, and -m:1 makes a rebuild safe. Ordering
         the two would take putting the analyzer into the outer graph, which is the project reference
         this file exists to avoid. -->
    <Target Name="_SlideRuleBuildTheAnalyzer" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
        <MSBuild Projects="$(_SlideRuleAnalyzerProject)"
                 Targets="Restore"
                 Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration);_SlideRuleAnalyzerRestore=true"
                 RemoveProperties="TargetFramework" />
        <MSBuild Projects="$(_SlideRuleAnalyzerProject)"
                 Targets="Build"
                 Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)"
                 RemoveProperties="TargetFramework" />
        <MSBuild Projects="$(_SlideRuleAnalyzerProject)"
                 Targets="GetSlideRuleAnalyzerClosure"
                 Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration)"
                 RemoveProperties="TargetFramework">
            <Output TaskParameter="TargetOutputs" ItemName="Analyzer" />
        </MSBuild>
    </Target>

    <!-- The package's own MSBuild file, imported rather than restated, so these builds exercise the wiring
         an adopter gets: the nearest sliderule.model.json, the baselines beside it, and the switch that
         says how much to report. -->
    <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../src/SlideRule.Analyzers/buildTransitive/SlideRule.Analyzers.targets" />

    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- No compiler server: it outlives the build that started it, and would go on holding the analyzer
             assemblies open under src/ where this repository's own build writes them. -->
        <UseSharedCompilation>false</UseSharedCompilation>
    </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
